ing context; do not invent reassurance. Accurate facts can still induce inappropriate stopping. Clinical review remains necessary."},"evidence_status":{"state":"retrieved","no_evidence_retrieved":false,"not_evidence_of_safety":true,"message":"Retrieved material is not a safety verdict; missing evidence is not evidence of safety."},"content_flags":{"dose_or_administration":{"detected":true,"field_paths":["/text","/sections/Summary of Use during Lactation","/sections/Drug Levels","/sections/Effects in Breastfed Infants","/sections/Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk","/interpretation_contract/measurements","/source_balance_context/text"],"path_format":"JSON Pointer relative to this payload","client_action":"Suppress flagged fields or visibly caveat them as historical source content, never personal instructions.","detection_limit":"Heuristic; an unflagged field is not certified free of dosing content."},"removed_pregnancy_letter_categories":{"detected":false,"matches":[],"classification_current":null,"instruction":"A/B/C/D/X pregnancy letter categories were removed by FDA PLLR. Preserve as historical source text only; never present as the current classification.","source_url":null}}},"patient_education_links":[{"title":"Metformin","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/druginfo/meds/a696005.html?utm_source=mplusconnect&utm_medium=service","summary":"Metformin is used to treat type 2 diabetes (condition in which the body does not use insulin normally and, therefore, cannot control the amount of sugar in the blood). Metformin is in a class of drugs called biguanides. Metformin helps to control the amount of glucose (sugar) in your blood. It decreases the amount of glucose you absorb from your food and the amount of glucose made by your liver. Metformin also increases your body's response to insulin, a natural substance that controls the amount of glucose in the blood.  Metformin is not used to treat type 1 diabetes (condition in which the body does not produce insulin and therefore cannot control the amount of sugar in the blood).","population_context":{"requested_population":null,"source_population":"not_established","source_basis":"Source MeSH/label section or original population field; missing stays unknown.","matches_requested_population_confirmed":false,"age_band_confirmed":false,"notice":"Search filters and broad population labels do not confirm applicability. Preserve actual age, prematurity, gestational stage and study population."}},{"title":"Diabetes Medicines","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/diabetesmedicines.html?utm_source=mplusconnect&utm_medium=service","summary":"<h3>What is diabetes?</h3>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/diabetes.html?utm_source=mplusconnect">Diabetes</a> is a disease in which your blood glucose, or <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/bloodglucose.html?utm_source=mplusconnect">blood sugar</a>, levels are too high. Glucose comes from the foods you eat. The cells of your body need glucose for energy. A hormone called insulin helps the glucose get into your cells.</p> 

With <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/diabetestype1.html?utm_source=mplusconnect">type 1 diabetes</a>, your body does not make insulin. With <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/diabetestype2.html?utm_source=mplusconnect">type 2 diabetes</a>,your body does not make or use insulin well. Without enough insulin, glucose can't get into your cells as quickly as usual. The glucose builds up in your blood and causes high blood sugar levels.<p></p>

<h3>What are the treatments for diabetes?</h3>
<p>Treatments for diabetes can depend on the type. Common treatments include a <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/diabeticdiet.html?utm_source=mplusconnect">diabetic meal plan</a>, regular physical activity, and medicines. Some less common treatments are <a href="https://medlineplus","population_context":{"requested_population":null,"source_population":"not_established","source_basis":"Source MeSH/label section or original population field; missing stays unknown.","matches_requested_population_confirmed":false,"age_band_confirmed":false,"notice":"Search filters and broad population labels do not confirm applicability. Preserve actual age, prematurity, gestational stage and study population."}}],"interpretation":{"medicine_taker":"Label adverse reactions are not automatically infant effects.","infant":"Use observed infant findings from LactMed; milk concentration alone is not a clinical outcome.","monitoring":"Source recommendations to monitor are not observations that a symptom occurred. Conversely, a symptom monitored in a study is not a clinical monitoring recommendation. Attribute advice only when explicitly present in the source.","measurements":"Keep measured milk/infant-serum concentrations separate from observed outcomes and administered dose regimens."},"questions_to_ask_clinician":["Does this evidence apply to my exact medicine, formulation, and all active ingredients?","How closely do the study populations match the lactation context, including age and prematurity where relevant?","Which findings measure exposure, and which measure clinical outcomes?","What uncertainties or missing evidence should we discuss before making a treatment decision?","What questions about benefits, risks, and follow-up should we address together?"],"questions_for_pharmacist":["Does this label match my exact product and formulation?","Which effects are documented in the person taking it versus a breastfed infant?","Which symptoms should prompt professional assessment?"],"limitations":["Source text can contain historical doses or treatment directives; do not turn these into personal instructions.","No diagnosis, dosing, schedule, taper, missed-dose advice or personal safety clearance. Missing sections are not reassuring evidence."],"continuity_of_care_notice":"Stopping or changing a medication can also carry risks. Discuss changes with the prescribing clinician. This tool cannot advise starting, stopping, or changing treatment.","framing_contract":{"preserve_source_balance_context":true,"clinical_framing_review_performed":false,"instruction":"When reporting adverse findings, retain relevant source context on treatment benefits, underlying disease and consequences of changing treatment if available. Disclose missing context; do not invent reassurance. Accurate facts can still induce inappropriate stopping. Clinical review remains necessary."},"retrieval_provenance":[{"host":"rxnav.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:28.934231-05:00","cache_hit":true,"source_version":"W/"20AA_261102F;1793130400;3.1.357""},{"host":"rxnav.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:29.021161-05:00","cache_hit":true,"source_version":"W/"20AA_261102F;1793130400;3.1.357""},{"host":"eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.126566-05:00","cache_hit":false,"source_version":"not_provided_by_upstream"},{"host":"dailymed.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.261999-05:00","cache_hit":false,"source_version":"not_provided_by_upstream"},{"host":"connect.medlineplus.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.342549-05:00","cache_hit":false,"source_version":"not_provided_by_upstream"},{"host":"eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.4314-05:00","cache_hit":false,"source_version":"not_provided_by_upstream"},{"host":"eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.4314-05:00","cache_hit":true,"source_version":"not_provided_by_upstream"},{"host":"dailymed.nlm.nih.gov","retrieved_at":"2026-11-21T15:07:31.550